  "inline_comments": [
    {
      "body": "![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)\n\nWhile it's great that you're moving to a dedicated logger for consistency, it's standard practice and more flexible to use `__name__` instead of `__file__` when initializing a logger. Using `__name__` creates a logger that's part of Python's module hierarchy (e.g., `verl.single_controller.ray.base`), which allows for powerful, hierarchical logging configuration (e.g., setting log levels for an entire submodule). Using `__file__` creates a logger named after the file path, which is less portable and breaks this hierarchical control.\n\n```suggestion\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\nlogger.setLevel(os.getenv(\"VERL_LOGGING_LEVEL\", \"WARN\"))\n```",
      "created_at": "2025-11-06T08:33:15Z",
      "diff_hunk": "@@ -29,6 +30,9 @@\n \n __all__ = [\"Worker\"]\n \n+logger = logging.getLogger(__file__)\n+logger.setLevel(os.getenv(\"VERL_LOGGING_LEVEL\", \"WARN\"))",
